 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Memory Overflow:** Standard 3DGS densification on large scenes causes memory overflow. TraGraph-GS mitigates this by training locally and avoiding densification in the global model [S1].
-*   **Boundary Artifacts:** Naive combination of local Gaussians causes floating artifacts or holes. The progressive rendering strategy with matching scores resolves this [S1].
-*   **Textureless Regions:** Without multi-view constraints, geometry reconstruction fails. The NCC-based $L_{mv}$ is critical here [S1].
+*   **Memory Overflow:** Naive partitioning can lead to unbalanced node distribution. The BFS method ensures balanced distribution to avoid this [S1].
+*   **Boundary Artifacts:** Combining all local Gaussians can cause floating artifacts or holes. The progressive rendering strategy with global context mitigates this [S1].
+*   **Textureless Regions:** Standard 3DGS struggles with geometry in textureless areas. The multi-view constraint $L_{mv}$ addresses this [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training is parallelized across regions, reducing per-GPU memory load but increasing total training time due to multiple optimization runs and global training. Rendering novel views requires sorting local Gaussians and progressive blending, adding overhead compared to standard 3DGS [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Significantly lower peak memory usage than monolithic 3DGS for large scenes, as local models are smaller and the global model is coarse and undensified [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Evaluated on large-scale aerial (Mill-19, UrbanScene3D) and ground-level datasets (SmallCity, Campus, Road, General) with thousands of images [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Handles arbitrary large-scale scenes; maintains real-time performance; improves texture in textureless regions; reduces boundary artifacts.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires initial feature matching and graph segmentation; parameter tuning for $k$ (regions) and $\beta$ (opacity threshold) is necessary [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Graph construction requires pairwise feature matching, which is $O(N^2)$ for $N$ images. Segmentation is linear in the number of edges and nodes. Local training is parallelized across regions. Global training is reduced due to downsampling and no densification.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Reduced compared to training a single global 3DGS model because local models are trained independently. Global model is coarse and downsampled.
+*   **Performance:** Evaluated on large-scale aerial and ground datasets (e.g., Rubble, Building, SmallCity). Maintains real-time performance across vastly different scales [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Handles arbitrary large-scale scenes, improves texture details in textureless regions, reduces boundary artifacts, and manages memory efficiently.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires initial feature matching and graph segmentation. Performance depends on the quality of feature matches for graph construction.
 
 ## Background
-TraGraph-GS was proposed to address the scalability issues of 3D Gaussian Splatting in large-scale environments. It builds upon the standard 3DGS framework [11] but introduces structural constraints via graph theory. The method was published in 2026 [S1].
+TraGraph-GS was proposed to address the challenges of rendering large-scale scenes with arbitrary camera trajectories, where standard 3DGS struggles with memory and boundary artifacts. It builds on the principles of 3D Gaussian Splatting [S1]. The method was published in IEEE Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence in 2026 [S1].
 
